// GAP (2026-07-14, seed rcdzc — RESOLVER, both surfaces): a NULLARY variant DOTTED pattern (`Ty.TInt`)
// in a NESTED match is resolved as a member-ACCESS expression, not a variant pattern:
//   error [CDZ0201]: member access requires a record — closest matches: `TInt`
// `cdz check` FAILS.
//
// SHARP BISECTION (RE-DIAGNOSED iter 25 — this is a RESOLVER bug, NOT ML-reader-only as first logged):
//   - The ML reader parses a nullary pattern `Ty.TInt` to the arena form `(. Ty TInt)` (a bare member
//     access), for BOTH the outer and inner match arms — IDENTICAL shape.
//   - At the OUTERMOST match, the resolver accepts that bare `(. Ty TInt)` as a nullary-ctor pattern.
//   - In a NESTED match (an arm body that is itself a match), it does NOT — it resolves the SAME bare
//     `(. Ty TInt)` as a `Resolved::Member` expression → "member access requires a record".
//   - The S-EXPR surface behaves IDENTICALLY: a bare `((match … ((. Ty TInt) …)))` nested arm ALSO fails
//     (my iter-24 "sexpr works" note was WRONG — that test used the DOUBLE-wrapped `((. Ty TInt))` form).
//   - The correctly-WRAPPED nullary pattern `((. Ty TInt))` (a zero-arg ctor application — an extra list
//     layer) works in BOTH nested and outer positions, on BOTH surfaces.
//   - The NON-nullary form `Ty.TInt(_)` (payload parens) works nested too.
// So the two intertwined facts: (a) the canonical nullary-ctor PATTERN shape is the WRAPPED `((. Ty
// TInt))`, and the ML reader emits the bare `(. Ty TInt)`; (b) the resolver's pattern-position handling
// tolerates the bare form at the OUTERMOST match arm but treats it as an expression in a NESTED match's
// arm. Either fix closes it: the ML reader should wrap a nullary ctor pattern, OR the resolver should
// treat a bare `(. T Ctor)` in ANY match-arm-pattern slot as a nullary-ctor pattern (as it does at top).
// WORKAROUND (used by `src/apply-ty.cdz`): a top-level `tag` helper maps each nullary variant to a scalar,
// so nullary comparison is `tag(x) == tag(y)` and no nested nullary match is needed.
//
// This program should return 9 (x is TInt → inner match on x → TInt arm → 9).
type Ty =
  | TInt
  | TBool

def g(x: Ty) = match x with
  | Ty.TBool => 0
  | Ty.TInt => (match x with
    | Ty.TBool => 8
    | Ty.TInt => 9)

export { g }
